//? Title: Freeze a trained affine predictor as TensorFlow GraphDef
//? Subtitle: Train, export, and reload a prediction-only graph
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Train a scalar affine predictor on five observations, export its learned
coefficients as GraphDef constants, and load the resulting graph for
inference. The exported graph accepts any batch length and keeps its
predictions after the live model changes.
)
\toc

> The method

For output f(x;theta), the recorded eager graph reveals which parameter tensors are connected
to that output. inference_parameters asks for VJPs with allow_unused: an undefined gradient
means disconnected; a defined zero gradient still means connected. Thus connectivity is not the
same as sensitivity at a single probe. The exported computation in this example is

$$
f(x)=wx+b,\qquad (w,b)=(2,-1/2),\qquad f((-3,1/2,4))=(-13/2,1/2,15/2).
$$
\alttext(The affine function with slope two and intercept minus one half gives predictions minus six point five, point five and seven point five.)

The affine exporter writes a placeholder, two scalar constants, and
the multiply and add operations. It exposes `features:0` and
`predictions:0` as the serving endpoints. Since the architecture is
specified by the exporter, the GraphDef needs only these five nodes
and carries no training state or device assignments. The eager
reachability helper demonstrated below instead discovers parameter
dependencies for an executed output.

The concept diagram below is drawn by `dm::graphics::Diagram`. Its editable C++
source is the complete block below; regeneration uses the same datamunge toolchain
as the numerical examples. Exports consume the saved local SVG.

```{cpp, file="figures/frozen_graph_concept.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  diagram.node("n0", {0.0, 0}, "Train eager model\nf(x)=wx+b");
  diagram.node("n1", {5.2, 0}, "Snapshot coefficients\nFloat32 constants");
  diagram.node("n2", {10.4, 0}, "Frozen GraphDef\nfeatures:0 → predictions:0");
  diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
  diagram.save_svg("build/frozen_graph_concept.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/frozen_graph_concept.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Train, export, and reload a prediction-only graph)
\alttext(An eager affine fit yields two scalar coefficients. These become constants in a GraphDef with named input and prediction endpoints.)

> Simple examples

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/frozen_graph_prediction.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <fstream>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::ParameterStore store;
  auto weight = store.get("w", {}, n::init::zeros());
  auto bias = store.get("b", {}, n::init::zeros());
  (void)store.get("unused", {8}, n::init::ones());
  const auto x = n::tensor({-2.f, -1.f, 0.f, 1.f, 2.f}, n::Shape{5}, n::DType::Float32);
  const auto y = n::tensor({-4.5f, -2.5f, -.5f, 1.5f, 3.5f}, n::Shape{5}, n::DType::Float32);
  const std::vector<n::Tensor> fitted{weight, bias};
  n::OptimizerConfig config;
  config.kind = n::OptimizerKind::Adam;
  n::Optimizer adam(fitted, .05, config);
  for (int i = 0; i < 500; ++i)
    adam.step(n::grad(n::mse(x * weight + bias, y), fitted));

  const auto query = n::tensor({-3.f, .5f, 4.f}, n::Shape{3}, n::DType::Float32);
  const auto expected = (query * weight + bias).to_vector<float>();
  const auto bytes = n::export_frozen_affine(weight, bias);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build/frozen_affine");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("figures");
  const std::string path = "build/frozen_affine/graph.pb";
  std::ofstream graph(path, std::ios::binary);
  graph.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(bytes.data()), bytes.size());
  graph.close();
  std::ofstream contract("build/frozen_affine/signature.txt");
  contract << "input=features:0 float32 [batch]\noutput=predictions:0 float32 [batch]\n";
  contract.close();
  if (!graph || !contract) throw std::runtime_error("Could not write GraphDef and signature");

  {
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    weight.copy_(n::full({}, -100., n::DType::Float32));
    bias.copy_(n::full({}, 100., n::DType::Float32));
  }
  const auto frozen = n::FrozenGraph::load(path);
  auto predict = [&](const n::Tensor& features) {
    return frozen.evaluate({{"features:0", features}}, {"predictions:0"}).front().second;
  };
  const auto prediction = predict(query);
  const auto one = predict(n::full({1}, 5., n::DType::Float32));
  const auto nodes = frozen.node_names();
  if (nodes != std::vector<std::string>{"model/w", "model/b", "features", "Multiply", "predictions"} ||
      frozen.operation("model/w") != "Const" || frozen.operation("model/b") != "Const" ||
      prediction.to_vector<float>() != expected || prediction.requires_grad() ||
      (query * weight + bias).to_vector<float>() == expected || std::abs(one.item() - 9.5) > 1e-5)
    throw std::runtime_error("Frozen model failed integrity, reload or dynamic batch checks");
  const auto actual = prediction.to_vector<double>();
  const std::vector<double> truth{-6.5, .5, 7.5};
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < truth.size(); ++i)
    if (std::abs(actual[i] - truth[i]) > 1e-5)
      throw std::runtime_error("Affine fit failed");

  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(query.to_vector<double>(), actual, "Frozen predictor")
      .points(x.to_vector<double>(), y.to_vector<double>(), "Training observations", {191, 88, 34}, 5)
      .title("Frozen affine fit after the live model changes").x_label("Feature").y_label("Prediction")
      .size(820, 420).save_svg("build/frozen_graph_prediction.svg");
  std::filesystem::copy_file("build/frozen_graph_prediction.svg", "figures/frozen_graph_prediction.svg",
                             std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
  std::println("GraphDef contains {} nodes and two constants. Reloaded predictions match exactly.", nodes.size());
  std::println("Predictions {:.5f}, {:.5f}, {:.5f}. Single-row prediction {:.5f}.",
               actual[0], actual[1], actual[2], one.item());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
GraphDef contains 5 nodes and two constants. Reloaded predictions match exactly.
Predictions -6.50000, 0.50000, 7.50000. Single-row prediction 9.50000.
\image(figures/frozen_graph_prediction.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The serialized prediction graph uses two scalar constants and accepts dynamic batches.)
\alttext(The serialized prediction graph uses two scalar constants and accepts dynamic batches.)

> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;n::ParameterStore store;
 const auto w=store.get("connected_zero",{},n::init::ones());store.get("unused",{},n::init::ones());
 const auto snapshot=n::inference_parameters(store,{w*0});
 if(snapshot.size()!=1||snapshot[0].first!="connected_zero"||snapshot[0].second.requires_grad())return 1;
 std::print("Zero derivative still retains connected parameter: {}.\n\nSnapshot requires_grad: {}.\n",snapshot[0].first,snapshot[0].second.requires_grad());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Zero derivative still retains connected parameter: connected_zero.

Snapshot requires_grad: false.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The fitted prediction graph is self-contained. Reloading it after
changing the live weights preserves the predictions, including on a
single row. The reachability example also shows how a zero derivative
can arise from a parameter that the executed computation still uses.

> References

- [TensorFlow: GraphDef constant conversion](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/compat/v1/graph_util/convert_variables_to_constants).
- [TensorFlow GraphDef schema|https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/blob/master/tensorflow/core/framework/graph.proto].
